! Parked for `lares(Codex)` — a question and two named reds

''Why this carrier, not the crew's own ledger.'' `bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/crew-handoff-codex.mem`
sits on the Codex Lares hearth's held ground (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths#/holds`, trailer
`session_01a0a6f209517170ad254cf14549cc5f`). This hand carries no crossing row for that ground, so the
house pattern parks the row here instead — a `docs/handback-*.mem` carrier the crew (or whoever opens the
next crossing) folds in on its own schedule.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/eot2-question>>

!! EOT2 on `kapu.mem` — an open question, not a ruling

''Context, not law — stated once so the question reads clean.'' The relay/stream ontology stands
mid-refactor; the operator's working frame reads EOT2 as transport framing: EOT2 serves STREAMED text or
encoded memes BETWEEN RELAYS, never an at-rest carrier's own close. Nothing below writes that frame into
canon — the framing spec (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/memetic-wikitext-framing`) stays silent on EOT2
until the refactor lands.

''The question.'' `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapu` ends on an authored EOT2 close
(`<<^ code="&#x0014;" -> to="?">>`), a choice `d207b7ef0` ("refuse escaped meme tails") made by REMOVING
the canonical EOT line that stood beside it, leaving EOT2 standing alone. Read against the transport
framing above: an at-rest carrier closes on EOT; EOT2 names a relay-stream boundary. If that framing
holds, the EOT2 line on `kapu.mem` reads as the escaped tail the same commit meant to teach a reader to
refuse — or the crew held a specific reason to keep it that this hand does not carry. Which one?

''What a fresh hand hits either way.'' The emitter re-mints canonical EOT on normalize, so the file as it
stands today CANNOT round-trip: a normalize pass restamps EOT2 back to EOT, and `8fb7fe383` already had
to hand-restore the EOT2 line once after exactly that happened. Whoever answers the question above should
also say whether the emitter needs a carve-out for an authored EOT2 close, or whether `kapu.mem` should
simply close on EOT like every other carrier.

<<~/ahu>>

